# Verbal Reasoning: Words, Statements, and Inference

> Verbal reasoning asks you to read a short piece of text and judge what
> it assumes, what it concludes, and what causes what — using only the
> words on the page. This opening lesson maps the sub-subject, shows how
> exams spread its marks, and builds the three core reading skills.

[important]
Every verbal reasoning topic runs on one discipline: the exact wording of
the given statement outranks your knowledge, intuition, and opinion. An
option that is true in the real world can still be wrong in the exam if
the statement itself does not say it.
[/important]

## 1. What Verbal Reasoning Tests

Verbal reasoning is the branch of reasoning built on *language* rather
than numbers, shapes, or diagrams. The exam gives a compact block of text
— a statement, a notice, an advertisement, a government policy, or a pair
of claims — and then asks you to pass a judgement on it.

| Question family | The judgement asked |
| --- | --- |
| Statement and Assumption | Is this hidden premise genuinely behind the statement? |
| Statement and Conclusion | Does this conclusion definitely follow from the text? |
| Cause and Effect | Is the first statement the cause of the second, the reverse, or neither? |
| Course of Action | Is this proposed step a sound, feasible response to the problem? |

Each question looks small; each punishes loose reading. A single word —
*only*, *all*, *some*, *may* — can flip the correct answer from one option
to another, so precision, not speed of reading, is what is being tested.

## 2. The Topic Map of This Sub-Subject

@diagram tree id="verbal-topics-tree" layout="tree" aria="Topic map of the verbal reasoning sub-subject"
  Verbal Reasoning -> Statement and Assumption
  Verbal Reasoning -> Statement and Conclusion
  Verbal Reasoning -> Cause and Effect
  Verbal Reasoning -> Course of Action
  Statement and Assumption -> Notices and circulars
  Statement and Assumption -> Advertisements
  Statement and Assumption -> Warnings and policies
  Statement and Conclusion -> Single statement
  Statement and Conclusion -> Two or more statements
  Cause and Effect -> Direct cause-effect pairs
  Cause and Effect -> Common-cause traps
  Course of Action -> Immediate relief steps
  Course of Action -> Long-term fixes
@/diagram

The four families share one skill — literal reading — but demand four
different judgements. Keep them separate in your head, because the same
sentence can be a *valid assumption* and an *invalid conclusion* at the
same time, depending on which question is asked about it.

[definition]
**Verbal reasoning**: reasoning performed on statements given in plain
language, where every judgement must be justified by the literal meaning
of the given text alone — no outside facts, no probabilities, no appeals
to what usually happens in life.
[/definition]

## 3. How Exams Distribute the Marks

In SSC, banking, and railway pattern papers, verbal judgement questions
typically appear as a fixed block inside the reasoning section. A
representative spread looks like this:

@chart bar title="Typical questions per verbal topic in a reasoning section"
labels:
Statement and Assumption
Statement and Conclusion
Cause and Effect
Course of Action
Statement and Argument

data:
4
5
3
3
2
@/chart

Two habits follow from this chart. First, the block is worth roughly
15 marks, so skipping it entirely is expensive. Second, Statement and
Conclusion usually carries the most questions, and it is also the topic
where elimination kills wrong options fastest — a double reason to master
it well.

[tip]
Attempt order inside the block: Cause and Effect and Course of Action
first, because their two-line statements deliver verdicts quickly; then
Statement and Assumption; then Statement and Conclusion, checking each
option one by one against the text.
[/tip]

## 4. Core Skill 1: Close Reading of Keywords

Before judging any option, underline the qualifiers — the words that fix
the strength of every claim in the statement and in the options.

| Keyword | Strength | What it actually licenses |
| --- | --- | --- |
| all, every, always, only | maximum | a universal claim; fails if a single exception exists |
| none, never | maximum negative | a universal denial |
| some, few, many, generally | moderate | at least one; does NOT deny the rest |
| may, might, can | weak | possibility only, never a certainty |
| should, ought to | advisory | a recommendation, not a fact |

[warning]
The three most misread words in verbal reasoning are *some* (it means
"at least one", not "some but not all"), *only* (it excludes everything
outside the term that follows it), and *may* (possibility is not
certainty). Options that quietly upgrade "may" into "will" are almost
always wrong.
[/warning]

## 5. Core Skill 2: Elimination Discipline

Verbal judgement questions are solved fastest backwards. Instead of
asking "which option is right?", test each option and give it one of
three verdicts:

1. **Supported** — the statement says this, at this strength or stronger.
2. **Contradicted** — the statement denies this outright.
3. **Out of scope** — the statement is silent; the option invents new
   facts, changes the strength, or reverses the direction of the claim.

An option can survive only as **Supported**. Any "Out of scope" or
"Contradicted" verdict eliminates it immediately, no matter how sensible
it sounds in the real world.

@diagram flowchart id="elimination-flow" layout="hierarchical" aria="Three-verdict elimination flow for verbal options"
node start label="Read the statement" shape="pill"
node test label="Test one option" shape="round-rect"
node q1 label="Does the statement say it?" shape="diamond"
node q2 label="Does the statement deny it?" shape="diamond"
node sup label="Supported" shape="round-rect"
node con label="Contradicted" shape="round-rect"
node scope label="Out of scope" shape="round-rect"

start -> test
test -> q1
q1 -> sup label="yes"
q1 -> q2 label="no"
q2 -> con label="yes"
q2 -> scope label="no, it is silent"
@/diagram

[tip]
When two options both survive, compare their strength: the option that
claims *more* than the statement does is nearly always the wrong one.
Verbal reasoning rewards the modest option, not the confident one.
[/tip]

[example]
Elimination in action — statement: "The museum opens at 10 a.m. on
weekdays."
- Option: "The museum opens before noon on weekdays." *Supported* —
  10 a.m. is before noon, a strictly weaker restatement.
- Option: "The museum opens at 10 a.m. on weekends." *Out of scope* —
  the statement is silent about weekends.
- Option: "The museum never opens on weekdays." *Contradicted* — the
  statement says the opposite.
Only the first option survives; the other two die without further
thought.
[/example]

## 6. Inference, Implication, Assumption, and Conclusion

These four words are not synonyms, and the exam relies on the
differences between them.

| Term | Where it lives | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Assumption | before the statement | the unstated premise the author must already believe |
| Implication | inside the statement | what the words suggest without saying directly |
| Inference | after the statement | a judgement you derive from what is written |
| Conclusion | after the statement | the claim that must be true if the statement is true |

Work one example through all four ideas: "You should carry an umbrella —
the forecast says rain."

- **Assumption**: the forecast is generally reliable (unstated, needed
  before the advice makes sense).
- **Implication**: the speaker expects rain (suggested, never said).
- **Inference**: rain is likely today (derivable from the forecast).
- **Conclusion**: carrying an umbrella today is advisable (the advice
  itself, restated as a followable claim).

[info]
Why this distinction earns marks: Statement and Assumption rejects options that merely restate the
conclusion; Statement and Conclusion rejects options that merely restate
an assumption. Mixing the two directions is the single most common way
candidates lose this block of marks.
[/info]

## 7. Solved Mini-Examples

**Example 1 (assumption vs conclusion).**
Statement: "The university has extended the library hours to midnight."
- Asked as an assumption — "The university expects students to need
  late-evening study time." *Implicit.* The extension is pointless
  unless the authority believes late demand exists.
- Asked as a conclusion — "All students will now study until midnight."
  *Does not follow.* Extended hours say nothing about what every
  student will actually do.

**Example 2 (keyword flip).**
Statement: "Only members may enter the reading room."
- Option: "Non-members may not enter the reading room." *Follows.*
  "Only members" excludes everyone else — this is the exact meaning.
- Option: "Members may not use the other rooms." *Out of scope.* The
  statement is silent about every other room in the building.

**Example 3 (temporal overlap is not causation).**
Statement: "Sales fell sharply in the quarter in which the price was
raised." Option: "The price rise caused the fall." *Not certain.* The
quarter only fixes the *time*; overlap in time is suggestive, but a
genuine cause-effect question supplies two complete statements to judge.

**Example 4 (strength change).**
Statement: "Regular exercise *may* reduce the risk of heart disease."
Option: "Regular exercise *will* reduce the risk of heart disease."
*Wrong.* The option upgrades "may" to "will"; a supported option must be
at the same strength as the statement or weaker.

## 8. Quick Revision

| Concept | One-line rule |
| --- | --- |
| Golden rule | Judge every option against the statement's exact wording only |
| Assumption | Unstated premise needed before the statement makes sense |
| Conclusion | Claim that must follow after the statement |
| Implicit vs explicit | Explicit is written down; implicit is hidden but required |
| Out of scope | Option adds facts the statement never gave — eliminate |
| Keyword "some" | At least one; never denies "all" |
| Keyword "only" | Excludes everything outside the named term |
| Keyword "may" | Possibility; never upgrade it to certainty |
| Elimination verdicts | Supported / Contradicted / Out of scope |
| Mark strategy | The verbal block is worth about 15 marks; elimination beats guessing |

[summary]
Verbal reasoning is literal reading under exam pressure: know the four
judgement families (assumption before, conclusion after, cause-effect
pairs, courses of action), read qualifiers ruthlessly, and eliminate any
option that adds information, changes strength, or reverses direction.
The next three lessons drill each family with fully solved questions.
[/summary]
